                <p>These are the groups we point people to most often. We aren't affiliated with any of them, and listing them here isn't an endorsement. Programs, dates and costs change, so check with each one directly. If we've missed one, or something here is out of date, please <a href="/contact.html">let us know</a>.</p>
                <p>Looking for someone to repair your watch? The directory lists businesses, including some repairers; for individual watchmakers, start with the AWCI.</p>

                <h2>Organizations</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.awci.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Watchmakers-Clockmakers Institute (AWCI)</a>, Harrison, Ohio. The professional association for watchmakers and clockmakers in the US, founded in 1934. It runs certification exams, classes and webinars, and accredits schools through its Research and Education Council.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.nawcc.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Association of Watch and Clock Collectors (NAWCC)</a>, Columbia, Pennsylvania. A nonprofit for collectors and anyone interested in horology, with local chapters, a research library, the National Watch and Clock Museum and the School of Horology.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://hs-ny.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horological Society of New York (HSNY)</a>, New York. A nonprofit founded in 1866 and an affiliate chapter of AWCI. It offers watchmaking classes, monthly lectures, and scholarships and grants for watchmaking students and independent watchmakers.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Watchmaking schools</h2>
                <p>Full-time programs that train people to service watches professionally. Some are run by colleges or nonprofits and are open to anyone; others are run by watch companies to train their own service watchmakers.</p>
                <h3>Colleges and independent schools</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://northseattle.edu/programs/watch-technology-institute" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch Technology Institute, North Seattle College</a>, Seattle, Washington. A public college program with a certificate and an associate degree.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.parisjc.edu/pathways/industry/horology.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horology at Paris Junior College</a>, Paris, Texas. Part of the college's Texas Institute of Jewelry Technology, teaching watchmaking since 1942, with certificates and an associate degree.</li>
                    <li><a href="http://www.gemcitycollege.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gem City College School of Horology</a>, Quincy, Illinois. A private trade school teaching watch and clock repair since 1961.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.yorktimeinstitute.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">York Time Institute</a>, York, Pennsylvania. A nonprofit school with a diploma program in the conservation, restoration and repair of watches and clocks, plus short courses.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://veteranswatchmakerinitiative.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Veterans Watchmaker Initiative</a>, Odessa, Delaware. A nonprofit that trains disabled veterans in watchmaking at no cost to students.</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>Run by watch companies</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.rolexwatchmakingtrainingcenter.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rolex Watchmaking Training Center</a>, Dallas, Texas. Run by Rolex.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.naiosw.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">North American Institute of Swiss Watchmaking</a>, Dallas, Texas. Run by Richemont.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.swatchgroup.com/en/career/nicolas-g-hayek-watchmaking-schools" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas G. Hayek Watchmaking School</a>, Miami, Florida. Run by the Swatch Group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Older lists of US schools may include the Lititz Watch Technicum in Pennsylvania and the watchmaking school at Oklahoma State University Institute of Technology, both of which have since closed. For other lists, see the <a href="https://hs-ny.org/watchmaking-schools-programs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horological Society of New York</a> and <a href="https://www.awci.com/educationcareers/research-and-education-council-rec-schools-u-s/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWCI</a>.</p>

                <h2>Classes and part-time study</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.awci.com/educationcareers/awci-course-catalog/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWCI classes</a>, in Harrison, Ohio, and online, from beginner courses to preparation for certification.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.nawcc.org/education/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAWCC School of Horology</a>, Columbia, Pennsylvania. Short hands-on workshops in watch and clock repair and machining.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://hs-ny.org/education" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HSNY classes</a>, New York. Hands-on introductory classes on how a mechanical watch works, taught by professional watchmakers; no experience needed.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Museums</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.nawcc.org/museum/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Watch and Clock Museum</a>, Columbia, Pennsylvania. Run by the NAWCC.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://clockandwatchmuseum.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Clock &amp; Watch Museum</a>, Bristol, Connecticut. A museum devoted to American clocks and watches, founded in 1952.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://elginhistory.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elgin History Museum</a>, Elgin, Illinois. Its largest exhibit covers the Elgin National Watch Company.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.charlesrivermuseum.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles River Museum of Industry &amp; Innovation</a>, Waltham, Massachusetts. On the industrial history of “Watch City,” including the Waltham Watch Company.</li>
                </ul>

